<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#state-peasant">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">State peasant</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as gosudarstvennye krestiane</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">State peasants were a social estate of the peasantry in 18th- and 19th-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> who lived on public land and paid taxes to the treasury; unlike private serfs they were considered personally free, though their freedom of movement was restricted. Created by decrees of Peter I, they grew through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a>'s confiscation of church lands, conquests and runaway serfs, from 19% of the population at the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> in 1724 to 45% at the last in 1858. They could appear in court, trade, own property and open factories. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pavel-kiselyov">Pavel Kiselyov</a>'s reforms of 1837–41 placed them under a new Ministry of State Property, and a law of 24 November 1866 let rural communities hold their land in ownership.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Peter I's decrees brought together under one estate a range of people who worked the land without belonging to a private lord: various peasant classes, the single homesteaders who served as military settlers on the border of the wild steppe, the non-serf Russian people of the Russian North, and the non-Russian peoples of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and the Ural regions. The crown peasants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> were probably the model for their legal position. By law they were free rural inhabitants, and the land they worked belonged to the state, though in practice they dealt in it as if they owned it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Besides church lands and conquests, the estate grew from the former serfs of confiscated estates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> of the Commonwealth, from runaway serfs, and from peasants allowed to settle untended lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkortostan">Bashkiria</a>, New Russia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-caucasus">North Caucasus</a>, a movement the government implicitly encouraged. In the second half of the 18th century it also handed hundreds of thousands of state peasants to the nobility, mainly in the western, formerly Polish provinces, and turned many into military settlers. Nobles proposed abolishing the estate and passing state land into private hands, but this was never done.</p><p>Most state peasants paid quit-rent in money to the state, together with a poll tax, local taxes and duties such as road building, while in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and the Baltic state estates were leased out and their peasants owed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a>.</p><p>Land shortage and rising dues in the early 19th century brought unrest, including the cholera riots and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a> riots of 1834 and 1840-41. Kiselyov's reform gave the new ministry guardianship over the peasants through the rural community, ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corvee-labour">corvée</a> and the leasing of state peasants, and modernised the dues. Kiselyov, a staunch opponent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>, wanted freedom introduced gradually; landowners resisted, fearing the example for their own peasants, and his plan to regulate private serfs' allotments was not carried out.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The drafters of the peasant reform of 1861 drew on Kiselyov's experience, above all in peasant self-government and the legal status of farmers. After the law of 1866, the redemption of holdings from the government was regulated by a law of 12 June 1886. The reforms cut state peasants' plots by 10% in the central provinces and 44% in the north. Payments were reckoned over 49½ years, but they were cancelled on 1 January 1907 as part of the Stolypin agrarian reform, under the influence of the revolution of 1905.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “State peasant” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The social hierarchy descended from the imperial family through the hereditary nobility (dvoryanstvo), the clergy, the urban merchants (kupechestvo), <b>state peasants</b> and privately owned serfs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the official count of 1857–1859 some 23 million were serfs on private estates, bound to the land and to their lords’ will, while about as many <b>state peasants</b> were personally free but tied to their communes.</blockquote><a href="/societies/russian-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Russian Empire</a></li></ul></section>
